In last week's we discussed the characteristics of the newly born offspring of

194
00:19:12,690 --> 00:19:18,670
several mammals. You probably remember that human infants are less developed

195
00:19:18,670 --> 00:19:22,170
physically than other mammals of the same age.

196
00:19:22,250 --> 00:19:28,170
But in today's today's lecture, we'll look at three very interesting studies that

197
00:19:28,170 --> 00:19:32,250
hint at the surprising abilities of human babies.

198
00:19:32,890 --> 00:19:39,530
In the first study, three -year -olds watched two videos shown side by side, each

199
00:19:39,530 --> 00:19:45,550
featuring a different researcher, one of whom they'd met once two years earlier.

200
00:19:45,930 --> 00:19:51,590
The children spent longer watching the video showing the researcher they hadn't met.

201
00:19:51,590 --> 00:19:58,450
This is consistent with young children's usual tendency to look longer at things

202
00:19:58,450 --> 00:19:59,670
that aren't familiar.

203
00:20:00,250 --> 00:20:02,590
And really, this is amazing.

204
00:20:03,010 --> 00:20:08,970
It suggests the children remembered the researcher they'd met just one time when

205
00:20:08,970 --> 00:20:11,010
they were only one -year -olds.

206
00:20:11,390 --> 00:20:16,890
Of course, as most of us forget memories from our first few years as we grow older,

207
00:20:16,890 --> 00:20:23,230
these early long -term memories will likely be lost in subsequent years.

208
00:20:24,090 --> 00:20:26,030
Our second study is about music.

209
00:20:26,590 --> 00:20:31,970
For this study, researchers played music to babies through speakers located on

210
00:20:31,970 --> 00:20:33,870
either side of human face.

211
00:20:34,490 --> 00:20:39,510
They waited until the babies got bored and averted their gaze from the face.

212
00:20:39,510 --> 00:20:45,830
And then they changed the mood of the music, either from sad to happy or the other

213
00:20:45,830 --> 00:20:53,130
way around. This mood switch made no difference to the three -month -olds, but for

214
00:20:53,130 --> 00:20:58,070
the nine -month -olds it was enough to renew their interest and they started looking

215
00:20:58,070 --> 00:21:00,430
again in the direction of the face.

216
00:21:01,030 --> 00:21:06,890
This suggests that babies of that age can tell the difference between a happy melody

217
00:21:06,890 --> 00:21:08,590
and a sad tune.

218
00:21:08,590 --> 00:21:14,890
Our final study is from 1980, but it's still relevant today.

219
00:21:15,170 --> 00:21:21,390
In fact, it's one of the most famous pieces of research about infant emotion ever

220
00:21:21,390 --> 00:21:28,330
published. The study involved ordinary adults watching video clips of babies nine

221
00:21:28,330 --> 00:21:29,590
months or younger.

222
00:21:29,990 --> 00:21:36,530
In the video clips, the babies made various facial expressions in response to real

223
00:21:36,530 --> 00:21:42,430
